{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Command the children of Israel, etc.</b> One needs to know what is the relevance of the commandment of oil and its kindling in this place, and likewise the commandment of the table's arrangement — what is its relevance here? All the more so since these matters were already stated as written in their place, in the commandments of the Tabernacle and its appurtenances. And Rashi, of blessed memory, explained what he explained, and there is nothing deficient in his words. And Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote that the oil was exhausted, etc., but there is no proof for his words." "Or it may be that since He commanded commandments of seven — Pesach seven days, the festival of Sukkot seven days, the festival joy of the musaf offerings seven days, and also Rosh HaShanah and Yom HaKippurim partake of the aspect of seven, in that they fall in the seventh month — therefore He juxtaposed to these as well the commandment of the Menorah, which is an aspect of seven lamps. Also the Table partakes of the sod of seven, as it is written ״שֵׁשׁ הַמַּעֲרָכֶת״ etc., and the loaves arranged upon it — it is the sod that gathers in it six levels, and it completes seven. And you will find that He commanded also in this parashah the commandment of the Omer, which is seven Sabbaths, and also the commandment of the Sabbath. You are therefore brought to say that He included all the commandments of seven together, to say that their sod and essential root is one." "<b>Further,</b> it is possible that what was intended by the juxtaposition of the parashah of the Menorah to the parashah of Sukkah is along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim), concerning the verse ״מִחוּץ לְפָרֹכֶת הָעֵדוּת״ — and this is their language: ״Now did He need it for light? But for all the forty years they were in the wilderness they journeyed only by its light.״ And the Tosafot explained (Shabbat 22b) that they did not travel by the light of the sun but by the light of the Shekhinah, for they could see what was inside the cloud-pillar, etc. Rather, it is a testimony to all the inhabitants of the world, etc.״ — end quote. And this is what HaShem, in His wisdom, intended by this juxtaposition of the commandment of the Menorah to the commandment of Sukkah, ״כִּי בַסֻּכּוֹת״ etc. — to say that Israel, on account of the clouds of glory, did not have the light of the sun, and their journeying was by His light, blessed be He. If so, the commandment of the Menorah is only for [the purpose stated in connection with] ״the curtain of the testimony״ — a testimony to all the inhabitants of the world, etc."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {1 "Reading מצות המנורה as referring to the seven lamps; the Table's 'sod of seven' follows his reading of the six loaves plus the Table itself completing seven."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:00:47.581333935Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}